  <!-- How it works -->
  <section id="how-it-works" aria-labelledby="how-title" class="border-t bg-muted/30">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 py-20 sm:px-8 sm:py-28">
      <h2 id="how-title" class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">How it works</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-[16px] leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
        Two protocols, one in each direction. ACP is how yaah runs the agent; MCP is how the agent calls back into yaah.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border bg-background p-4 sm:p-8">
        <Diagram />
      </div>
      <div class="mt-12 grid gap-x-10 gap-y-8 text-[15px] leading-7 text-muted-foreground md:grid-cols-3 [&_code]:rounded-sm [&_code]:bg-muted [&_code]:px-1 [&_code]:font-mono [&_code]:text-[0.86em] [&_code]:text-foreground">
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-medium text-foreground">ACP runs the agent</h3>
          <p class="mt-2">
            yaah starts the agent's process and speaks JSON-RPC to it over stdio: sessions, prompts, streamed replies, tool calls and
            permission requests. One process per account, many chats per process.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-medium text-foreground">MCP brings the agent back</h3>
          <p class="mt-2">
            Each chat gets an HTTP MCP server on <code>127.0.0.1</code> with its own secret token, handed to the agent when the session starts.
            Its tools: <code>ui_catalog</code>, <code>ui_guide</code>, <code>ui_get_component</code>, <code>ui_render</code>, <code>ui_ask</code>, <code>ui_get_values</code>,
            <code>ui_set_values</code>, <code>ui_save_component</code>, <code>ui_request_folders</code> and <code>source_read</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-medium text-foreground">Rust owns the logic</h3>
          <p class="mt-2">
            The Rust core keeps the transcripts and pushes each change to the interface, which only renders. Chats are saved to
            <code>~/.yaah/sessions/</code> and picked up again when yaah restarts.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Get started -->
  <section id="get-started" aria-labelledby="start-title" class="border-t">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 pt-20 sm:px-8 sm:pt-28">
      <h2 id="start-title" class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Get started</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-[16px] leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
        One command installs the latest release on an Apple Silicon Mac or on Linux (x86_64 or arm64).
      </p>
      <Command class="mt-8 w-fit max-w-full" command={INSTALL} label="Install the latest release" />
    </div>
    <div class="mx-auto mt-10 grid max-w-6xl gap-10 px-5 sm:px-8 md:grid-cols-12 md:gap-12">
      <div class="md:col-span-5">
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-[15px] leading-7 text-muted-foreground marker:text-border [&_code]:rounded-sm [&_code]:bg-muted [&_code]:px-1 [&_code]:font-mono [&_code]:text-[0.86em] [&_code]:whitespace-nowrap [&_code]:text-foreground">
          <li>It downloads the build for your system and the release's <code>SHA256SUMS</code>, and stops if the SHA-256 doesn't match.</li>
          <li><strong class="font-medium text-foreground">macOS:</strong> <code>yaah.app</code> goes in <code>/Applications</code>, or <code>~/Applications</code> when that isn't writable.</li>
          <li>
            <strong class="font-medium text-foreground">Linux:</strong> the AppImage goes in <code>~/.local/bin/yaah</code>, with a menu entry and icon. With
            <code>--deb</code> (Debian, Ubuntu) it installs the <code>.deb</code> through apt instead.
          </li>
          <li>It runs nothing it downloads except the installed app. <code>--dry-run</code> shows what it would do.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-6 text-[15px] leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
          To chat, you need Node with npm (the npm-based agents run with it) and an account with the agent you use, such as a
          logged-in Claude Code.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="min-w-0 space-y-6 md:col-span-7">
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Installer options">
          <table class="w-full text-[14px] leading-6">
            <caption class="border-b bg-muted/60 px-3.5 py-2 text-left font-mono text-[12px] text-muted-foreground">
              Options go after <span class="text-foreground">sh -s --</span>, e.g. <span class="text-foreground">… | sh -s -- --dry-run</span>
            </caption>
            <tbody>
              {
                installOptions.map(([flag, what]) => (
                  <tr class="border-b last:border-b-0">
                    <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 pl-3.5 text-left align-top font-mono text-[12.5px] font-normal whitespace-nowrap text-foreground">{flag}</th>
                    <td class="py-2 pr-3.5 text-muted-foreground">{what}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border px-4 py-3.5 text-[15px] leading-7 text-muted-foreground [&_code]:rounded-sm [&_code]:bg-muted [&_code]:px-1 [&_code]:font-mono [&_code]:text-[0.86em] [&_code]:text-foreground">
          <p>
            <strong class="font-medium text-foreground">macOS builds are ad-hoc signed.</strong> They aren't signed with an Apple Developer ID or
            notarised yet; notarised builds are planned. The installer verifies the checksum, then removes the quarantine attribute so yaah
            opens without a Gatekeeper prompt.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-2">
            Downloaded the <code>.dmg</code> with a browser instead? macOS will say it can't verify the developer: run
            <code>xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/yaah.app</code>, or allow it in System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[15px] leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
          Or download from <a href={RELEASES} class="text-foreground underline decoration-border underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-current">Releases</a>:
          the <code class="rounded-sm bg-muted px-1 font-mono text-[0.86em] text-foreground">.dmg</code> for macOS, the AppImage or
          <code class="rounded-sm bg-muted px-1 font-mono text-[0.86em] text-foreground">.deb</code> for Linux, and
          <code class="rounded-sm bg-muted px-1 font-mono text-[0.86em] text-foreground">SHA256SUMS</code> to check them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto mt-16 grid max-w-6xl gap-10 px-5 pb-20 sm:mt-20 sm:px-8 sm:pb-28 md:grid-cols-12 md:gap-12">
      <div class="border-t pt-10 md:col-span-5">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Build from source</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
          To change yaah, or to try its interface in a browser. Clone
          <a href={REPO} class="text-foreground underline decoration-border underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-current">the repository</a> first.
        </p>
        <h4 class="mt-6 font-medium">You need</h4>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-[15px] leading-7 text-muted-foreground marker:text-border">
          <li>Rust</li>
          <li>Node, with npm and npx, for the agents that come from npm</li>
          <li>A logged-in Claude Code account, to chat with Claude Code</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
          The versions are pinned in <code class="rounded-sm bg-muted px-1 font-mono text-[0.86em] text-foreground">app/mise.toml</code>, and
          <code class="rounded-sm bg-muted px-1 font-mono text-[0.86em] text-foreground">mise install</code> sets them up.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="min-w-0 space-y-6 md:col-span-7 md:border-t md:pt-10">
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-lg border bg-muted/60">
          <div class="border-b px-3.5 py-2 font-mono text-[12px] text-muted-foreground">Run the app</div>
          <pre class="overflow-x-auto px-3.5 py-3 font-mono text-[13px] leading-[1.7] focus-visible:-outline-offset-2" tabindex="0"><code>git clone {REPO}.git
cd yaah/app
pnpm install
pnpm tauri dev</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-lg border bg-muted/60">
          <div class="border-b px-3.5 py-2 font-mono text-[12px] text-muted-foreground">Or only the interface, in a browser</div>
          <pre class="overflow-x-auto px-3.5 py-3 font-mono text-[13px] leading-[1.7] focus-visible:-outline-offset-2" tabindex="0"><code>cd yaah/app
pnpm dev
<span class="text-muted-foreground"># open http://localhost:1420</span></code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[15px] leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
          The browser mock answers the app's commands with recorded fixtures, so you can try the interface without the native app or an
          agent. It's what the screenshots on this page were taken from.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

